  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 AM EDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThe majority of this discussion will focus on now through late \nTuesday.\n\nNow or This Morning:\n\nLast night and into the early morning hours today, we monitored \nan MCS moving south out of KY. It generated severe thunderstorm \nwatches for middle TN, but that threat never extended east from \nthere. What's left of a broken apart and decaying MCS, is what's \ncurrently exiting the northern plateau this morning. Severe storms\nare not anticipated. At worst, strong wind gusts up to 40 mph are\npossible with heavy rain and lightning. The storms are moving at a\ndecent pace and aren't back-building, so a flooding threat is\npretty low.\n\nTonight into Tomorrow Morning:\n\nIt should be a mostly dry day today before the next possible round \nof showers and storms tonight and into tomorrow morning. Another \nnorth to south moving MCS seems plausible, paired with pre-frontal \nshowers and storms shifting out of KY and WV during the morning \nhours. Various forecast soundings around 12z tomorrow depict either \na marginal threat for severe or none at all dependent on timing and \nif there's sufficient surface instability. Northeast TN into \nsouthwest VA may experience the greatest impacts, if any, with\ndamaging winds the primary hazard.\n\nTomorrow Afternoon into Tomorrow Evening (Tuesday):\n\nModels are coming more in agreement that we may see impacts from the \ncold front much earlier in time, when a couple days ago it was \nthought to be an overnight event. Though development and arrival \nwill most likely be during the \"best\" time of day for strong to \nsevere storms or peak heating, how it ends up playing out will all \nbe dependent on the morning activity and how much clearing and \ndestabilizing we can do. The greatest threat will be damaging \nwind gusts with heavy rains and possible flooding. Once we get \ncloser and are able to see how early tomorrow may unfold, we can \ndetermine if secondary hazards like tornadoes and large hail will \nneed considered as well. Timing as of this moment will be as early\nas the later morning hours to as late as the evening time frame. \nSo, most likely time of greatest chances/threat will be the \nafternoon into the early evening.\n\nBefore getting into the rest of the forecast period, which won't be \nmuch to touch on at all, it will be hot and humid later today and \nespecially tomorrow. Southwest VA into northeast TN may escape the \nhottest conditions, as well as the Southern Appalachians, but \nthat goes without saying. Mid to upper 90s and maybe some 100s \nsprinkled in for heat indices today from the central valley south \nto the GA border. Better chances for low 100s heat indices for \ntomorrow afternoon with highs in the low to mid 90s.\n\nWednesday into the first weekend of August will see temperatures\ndropping to just below normal and for the most part, holding\nsteady as lower heights from the western side of the trough sink\nsouth. Humidity will also trend lower into Thursday before a trend\nupward begins Friday. Surprisingly enough, once we clear things\nout of here early Wednesday, dry weather will most likely persist\ninto early Saturday before precipitation chances creep back in.\nSurface high pressure at that time, begins to be replaced by\nanother shortwave lobe and a system possibly moving in from the \nNW.",
